[[Open This|2.2 Disks, snapshots, and images]]

---

https://docs.oxide.computer/guides/key-entities-and-concepts#_disks_snapshots_and_images

Disks are persistent volumes that can be managed independently from VM instances. A snapshot is a lightweight point-in-time copy of a disk that can be used to create a reusable image shared within the project or with other projects. New disks can be created from snapshots or images of existing ones, enabling disk cloning and templating.

Oxide racks do not provide guest operating system images out of the box. Images must be imported from an external file. Technically, the local image file is imported into a disk, then the disk is snapshotted, and finally an image resource is created from the snapshot. The web console and CLI conveniently combine these steps into a single command. See the Images and Disks and Snapshots guides for more information.
